-- allium: 3
-- startup.allium
--
-- Scope: What happens between the operator typing `dispatch tui` and the
--        kanban board drawing its first frame.
-- Includes: the tmux session the board requires and how the command supplies
--           one for itself; what becomes of a board already running in that
--           session; the configuration drift check that runs once the
--           board's session exists; and the three outcomes of that check
--           (silent, prompted, reported); and the host-label gate that runs
--           after it — whether this machine has been named, the prompt that
--           asks when it has not, the abort when nobody can be asked, and the
--           abort when the store that identity lives in cannot be read, minted
--           into, or written back to; and the shared store the board is
--           mandatory on — the managed local store the board brings up, adopts
--           and stops for itself when no store is named, the embedded module it
--           publishes there when the store's copy is stale, and the first
--           connection, made before the board draws, whose failure aborts too.
-- Excludes: everything the board does after it draws (see board-layout.allium,
--           tasks.allium); what a dispatched agent's own tmux window contains
--           (see dispatch.allium); what configuration setup actually writes,
--           which is dispatch.allium's and observability.allium's subject —
--           this spec governs only *when* it is written and *who is asked*.
--           ONE exception, and it is about derivation rather than content: the
--           caller-headers helper recorded in the MCP entry must not vary with
--           which build ran the check, nor with the shell that launched it,
--           because a value that varies makes the drift check disagree with
--           itself. That is this spec's own ConfigDriftEngine contract talking,
--           so it is stated there — see TheHelperIsTheBareCommandName.
--           The same line is drawn for the host label: what a label may be,
--           that it is refused when empty after trimming, and the single rule
--           that writes it are host.allium's (RenameHost) — this spec governs
--           only when the operator is asked for one and what happens when they
--           cannot be.
--           Also excluded: seeding the example feed epic (see feeds.allium),
--           which adds an epic row to dispatch's own shared store — not a file
--           in the operator's configuration — and so is not a configuration
--           artefact and is not gated on consent. It happens once the board's
--           first store connection is up (a created row needs the settled
--           identity — sync.allium: CreatesRequireASettledIdentity), not here;
--           doing it beside the consent prompt would come before any
--           connection exists, and would leave a fresh store unseeded on every
--           machine whose configuration was already current.
--
--           THE SHIPPED FEED SCRIPTS ARE THE EXCEPTION TO THAT REASONING, and
--           are a configuration artefact (`feed_scripts`) despite living in the
--           same data directory. The line the exclusion above is really drawing
--           is not which directory a write lands in but whose property it is.
--           Seeding adds an epic row that was not there. Installing the feed
--           scripts can REPLACE a file the operator edited, which is the one
--           thing on either side of that line that consent exists for. What is
--           written, and the second question asked before any overwrite of a
--           script whose provenance dispatch cannot prove, is feeds.allium's
--           subject (`InstallShippedFeedScripts`); this spec governs only that
--           it is reported in the drift report and gated on the same consent.
--
-- Why this exists: getting started used to be three commands in a fixed order
-- that nothing stated — install, `dispatch setup`, start a tmux server, then
-- `dispatch tui`. Two of those are preconditions the board can satisfy for
-- itself, and the failure mode for each was an error message rather than an
-- action. This spec collapses the sequence to one command without making the
-- board write to the operator's home directory behind their back: the tmux
-- session is provided silently because it is dispatch's own, and configuration
-- is only ever written after the operator says yes.
--
-- The session the command supplies outlives the board that ran in it. tmux
-- keeps it alive as long as any agent window remains, so the next
-- `dispatch tui` meets a session that already exists and a board window that
-- may hold anything: a live board, a board built from a binary two hours old,
-- or a pane sitting at a shell prompt where one exited. Attaching to that
-- session and calling it a launch is the one outcome the operator did not ask
-- for — they typed the command that starts the board, so the board starts.
--
-- The store followed the same arc (task #12296). Once it became mandatory the
-- getting-started sequence grew back to three commands — `spacetime start`,
-- `spacetime publish -p spacetime/module`, then `dispatch tui --spacetime-server
-- <url>` — each a precondition the board could satisfy for itself on a machine
-- where nobody named a store. It now does, on loopback only, and never for a
-- store the operator named: that one may be somebody else's.

use "./core.allium" as core

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Value Types
------------------------------------------------------------

-- What the board found out of date when it checked, one entry per managed
-- artefact. An empty report is the normal steady state and is the only case
-- that produces no output at all.
value ConfigDrift {
    items: List<ConfigArtefact>

    is_clean: items.count = 0
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Contracts
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The drift check and the apply step are two halves of one thing and must
-- agree about what "out of date" means. They are stated together because a
-- disagreement between them is silent in both directions: an artefact the
-- check calls stale and the writer calls current is reported as needing an
-- update forever, and the reverse leaves a stale artefact permanently
-- invisible.
contract ConfigDriftEngine {
    -- Every managed artefact whose on-disk state differs from what this build
    -- would write. Reads only.
    inspect_config_drift: () -> ConfigDrift

    -- Bring every artefact in the report up to date. Writes. Asks nothing,
    -- with exactly one stated exception (see AskingBelowTheConsentPrompt).
    -- Yields the artefacts it could not write, empty when all succeeded.
    apply_config_update: (drift: ConfigDrift) -> List<ConfigArtefact>

    @invariant InspectWritesNothing
        -- inspect_config_drift only reads. It creates no directory and no
        -- file, not even an empty one, so an operator who declines the prompt —
        -- or who is never asked — ends the run with their configuration
        -- byte-identical to how it started.

    @invariant OneDefinitionOfOutOfDate
        -- inspect_config_drift calls an artefact stale exactly when
        -- apply_config_update would change its bytes. Neither half may hold a
        -- predicate the other does not.

    @invariant ApplyIsIdempotent
        -- apply_config_update against a clean report changes nothing, and a
        -- second inspect_config_drift immediately after an apply reports no
        -- further drift. The pair converges rather than flip-flopping.

    @invariant AskingBelowTheConsentPrompt
        -- apply_config_update asks nothing, with ONE exception: the
        -- feed_scripts artefact asks before overwriting a shipped script whose
        -- provenance it cannot prove (feeds.allium:
        -- InstallShippedFeedScripts). It is an exception rather than a second
        -- general permission, and it is narrow on three counts. It fires only
        -- on that artefact, only for a file that differs from BOTH the shipped
        -- content and the digest dispatch last recorded for it, and never when
        -- nobody can answer.
        --
        -- Why the report's single consent does not already cover it. That
        -- consent is one decision for a list the operator was shown, and it is
        -- safe to take as one decision because every other artefact writes
        -- something dispatch owns — a stale MCP entry, an old plugin tree, a
        -- statusline file — where the worst case is a configuration they can
        -- re-derive. This branch replaces a file the OPERATOR wrote. There is
        -- nothing to re-derive it from, so it is the one write in this spec
        -- whose cost the drift report cannot state on their behalf.
        --
        -- It defaults to NO, and the previous content is copied aside first,
        -- so the destructive answer is never the one reached by pressing enter
        -- and never the one reached by silence.

    @invariant TheHelperIsTheBareCommandName
        -- The caller-headers helper recorded in the mcp_server_entry artefact
        -- is a FIXED STRING — the bare command name and its subcommand. It
        -- names no directory. Nothing about the run that writes it can reach
        -- the value: not the binary that is running, not the operator's PATH,
        -- not the working directory. Resolution to a file on disk is Claude
        -- Code's, done under Claude Code's own PATH at the moment it invokes
        -- the command.
        --
        -- Why nothing may reach the value. The helper entry outlives the run
        -- that wrote it, and the runs that reach this check are not all the
        -- operator's installed one. A build from a worktree reaches it on the
        -- ordinary board-launch path, because there is no separate setup
        -- command left to keep it away. Recording the RUNNING BINARY let such a
        -- run write a path inside that worktree, which stops existing the
        -- moment the worktree is removed. The entry was then live and broken,
        -- nothing re-derived it until the operator next ran their installed
        -- binary, and nothing had reported a failure — the run that wrote it
        -- succeeded. Pointing the run at a throwaway database does not avoid
        -- this: that redirects dispatch's own data directory, while the
        -- operator's Claude Code configuration is resolved separately and is
        -- reached all the same.
        --
        -- Looking the command up on the operator's PATH at startup closed that
        -- build axis but left a second one. The answer was then the first
        -- installed binary the LAUNCHING SHELL's PATH named, so two boards
        -- started from shells that order PATH differently recorded different
        -- values and each found the other's entry stale — a rewrite prompt for
        -- a difference that is not drift in either direction, which is what
        -- OneDefinitionOfOutOfDate and ApplyIsIdempotent need to be free of. A
        -- fixed string has neither axis, by construction rather than by
        -- argument.
        --
        -- THE STATUS LINE IS THE PRECEDENT, not a counter-example. The
        -- status_line artefact records a bare `dispatch` invocation too, and
        -- always has. Both artefacts are command strings the SAME Claude Code
        -- process runs, under the same PATH, so a rule that held for one and
        -- not the other would have to name a difference between them. There is
        -- none. The two now agree, and this invariant is the statement that
        -- their agreeing is deliberate.
        --
        -- What is given up, stated rather than glossed: where Claude Code's own
        -- PATH holds no dispatch, the helper cannot be invoked and no caller
        -- identity reaches the board. That is accepted on two counts. Claude
        -- Code's PATH is the one the command actually runs under, so it is the
        -- right one to decide — which is the same reason the launching shell's
        -- was the wrong one. And the cost is small: the helper emits one
        -- constant payload naming a NON-DISPATCHED session, and a dispatched
        -- agent never reaches it, because its launch overrides this entry with
        -- one carrying a fixed caller-task header (dispatch.allium:
        -- AgentCarriesItsOwnCallerIdentity).
        --
        -- The entry's address is deliberately NOT held to this. It names the
        -- board the operator is starting, port and all, which is what a launch
        -- on a different port is asking for. The helper is not: it identifies a
        -- Claude Code session to whichever board it reaches, and no run has a
        -- reason to want its own build, its own shell or its own port named
        -- there.

    @invariant PartialFailureIsStillProgress
        -- One artefact that cannot be written does not abandon the rest. Each
        -- is attempted; the outcome names those that failed.

    @invariant OnlyTheReportedArtefactsAreWritten
        -- apply touches the artefacts the report names and no others. It does
        -- not re-derive whether each one is stale: inspect already decided, and
        -- asking again would read every file and spawn every subprocess a
        -- second time to reach the same answer. This does not weaken
        -- OneDefinitionOfOutOfDate — the shared predicate is still the only
        -- definition, now evaluated once rather than twice.

    @guidance
        -- The two halves belong to the artefact rather than to the engine: each
        -- artefact owns both its "is it current?" reading and its write, so the
        -- pair is one unit a reader sees together and a new artefact cannot
        -- acquire one half without the other. An engine holding two separate
        -- lists — one of readers, one of writers — upholds
        -- OneDefinitionOfOutOfDate only by each author remembering to add to
        -- both.
}

-- The tmux seam the launch path drives. Stated as a contract because every
-- operation in it is answered by a subprocess that can fail for reasons this
-- spec does not enumerate, and because the board's own tmux usage (windows,
-- panes, keybindings — see dispatch.allium and split-pane.allium) starts only
-- after this contract has produced a session.
contract SessionLauncher {
    -- Whether this process is already inside a tmux session.
    inside_tmux_session: () -> Boolean

    -- Whether a session of this name exists right now.
    session_exists: (session: String) -> Boolean

    -- Whether the launching process is a shell sharing the board's window with
    -- a board that is already there — the one case where retiring that window
    -- would take the operator's own shell down with it.
    --
    -- Not simply "is this process in a window carrying
    -- config.board_window_name". The board dispatch has just started is in
    -- exactly such a window: both entry paths name it at creation, and the
    -- board's own process then runs the launch path like any other invocation.
    -- A predicate that only read the window's name would have every board
    -- refuse its own launch.
    --
    -- The board holds its window alone. A second pane in it is therefore
    -- somebody else — the split-pane agent, or the operator's shell beside it —
    -- and that, not the name, is what this asks.
    launching_from_the_board_window: () -> Boolean

    -- Whether this process is the board tmux has just started, alone in a
    -- window created for it and carrying config.board_window_name.
    --
    -- The complement of the predicate above over the same two readings, and the
    -- reason both exist: a window bearing the board's name is either one this
    -- launch is about to fill or one somebody else already fills, and the two
    -- call for opposite actions.
    launching_as_the_started_board: () -> Boolean

    -- Whether tmux will say which session this process is inside. False is not
    -- the same as "no session" — it means the question could not be answered,
    -- and there is then no session name to scope a retire to. See
    -- RefuseWhenTheSessionCannotBeIdentified.
    session_is_identified: () -> Boolean

    -- Close the board's window in `session`, whatever it currently holds.
    -- The outcome distinguishes the three states the session can be left in,
    -- because the launch path does something different in each.
    retire_board_window: (session: String) -> RetireOutcome

    -- Put this process inside the named session, creating it when absent and
    -- attaching when present, running `argv` inside it. Yields nothing on
    -- success because there is no "after" — the process is gone. A failure
    -- names which of the two it was.
    enter_session: (session: String, argv: List<String>) -> SessionLaunchFailure?

    -- Retire the board in `session`, start a fresh one from `argv` in a new
    -- window of that same session, and attach to it. One operation rather than
    -- three, because the steps are not independently useful: a retire with no
    -- replacement leaves the operator with no board, and a replacement with no
    -- retire leaves two. Yields nothing on success — this process is gone.
    --
    -- Its failures are the full abort set rather than the tmux seam's two: a
    -- board that would not close is a way this can fail that entering a session
    -- has no counterpart for.
    restart_in_session: (session: String, argv: List<String>) -> StartupAbortReason?

    @invariant RetiringTouchesOnlyTheBoardWindow
        -- retire_board_window closes the board's window and nothing else. The
        -- agent windows, editor windows and shells beside it in the session
        -- are left running, and the session itself is discarded only by tmux's
        -- own rule that a session with no windows ceases to exist. A restart
        -- that took the agents down with the board would make restarting cost
        -- more than the stale board it fixes.

    @invariant RetiringAnAbsentBoardWindowSucceeds
        -- A session with no board window — the operator closed it, or the
        -- board crashed and the window went with it — is already in the state
        -- retiring aims at. It is not an error and not reported as one.

    @invariant AFailedRetireIsNotMistakenForSuccess
        -- A board window that is still there after the attempt is reported as
        -- such, distinctly from one that was never there. The two look alike
        -- to a caller that only asks whether the session survived, and the
        -- launch that follows them differs completely: one starts the
        -- replacement board, the other would put a second board beside a live
        -- one.

    @invariant RetiredMeansGoneNotSignalled
        -- retire_board_window reports success only once the retired board has
        -- actually gone, not once tmux has been told to close its window. The
        -- two are different moments: closing a window signals the process in
        -- it, and that process releases the agent port when it exits, some time
        -- afterwards.
        --
        -- "Gone" means the board's process, not tmux's pane listing (which
        -- forgets the pane the moment its window is killed), and the wait is
        -- longer than config.managed_store_stop_timeout, because the retired
        -- board stops its managed store before it exits.
        --
        -- A launch that raced the gap reached the port claim first and told the
        -- operator another board was holding the port — seconds after the same
        -- launch had closed it. Waiting is bounded; a board still there at the
        -- end is reported as board_window_survived, which names the board
        -- rather than the port.
        --
        -- This does not reintroduce a retry on the port itself
        -- (`AbortWhenTheAgentPortIsTaken` still fails rather than waiting).
        -- The wait belongs here, where the launch knows exactly which process
        -- it is waiting for and why, rather than at the port, where a board
        -- mid-exit and a board the operator still wants are indistinguishable.

    @invariant RetiringIsNotConditionalOnLiveness
        -- retire_board_window does not ask whether the board in the window is
        -- running, responsive or recent. There is one launch behaviour, not
        -- one per guess about the previous board's health: a liveness check
        -- that answers wrongly produces exactly the stale board the operator
        -- ran the command to replace.

    @invariant EnteringReplacesThisProcess
        -- Entering a session replaces this process rather than spawning a
        -- child beside it. The operator's shell waits on the tmux client, so
        -- detaching from the board returns them to their prompt with nothing
        -- left running behind it.

    @invariant NeverCreatesASecondSession
        -- A session that already exists is attached to, never duplicated under
        -- a suffixed or derived name. This is what upholds
        -- ExactlyOneBoardPerSession. Create-or-attach is one indivisible
        -- operation rather than a check followed by a create: between the two
        -- halves of a split version, a session the check did not see could
        -- appear, and the create would then be the second board the invariant
        -- forbids.

    @invariant ArgvIsCarriedThrough
        -- argv is the invocation being relocated, not a fresh default one.
        -- Whatever database path and port the operator named reach the board
        -- that comes up inside the session. This holds on both entry paths: a
        -- session created for the launch runs argv in its first window, and a
        -- session that already existed gets a new window running the same
        -- argv. The operator's arguments never fall back to the ones a
        -- previous board happened to be started with.
        --
        -- One exception, and it is a race rather than a rule: enter_session
        -- creates-or-attaches indivisibly (`NeverCreatesASecondSession`), so a
        -- session that appeared between the launch reading session_exists and
        -- the call itself is attached to, and argv is not run. The launch then
        -- lands on a board it did not start. Accepted over the alternative,
        -- which is a second session under a derived name — a rival board is a
        -- worse outcome than the operator's own board coming up a moment
        -- before this invocation asked for it.

    @invariant DistinguishesAbsentFromBroken
        -- A tmux that is not installed and a tmux that refused are reported as
        -- different failures. The operator's next action differs: install it,
        -- or look at why the server rejected the request.
}

-- The seam to the store dispatch runs for itself when the launch names none.
-- Stated as a contract for the same reason SessionLauncher is: every operation
-- is answered by the `spacetime` CLI or by a server on the managed address,
-- and either can fail for reasons this spec does not enumerate. None of it is
-- consulted when the launch named a store (explicit_store_server() != null) —
-- see ANamedStoreIsNeverManaged.
contract ManagedStoreHost {
    -- The store the operator named on this launch: --spacetime-server, else
    -- DISPATCH_SPACETIME_SERVER, else null. A blank value is null too:
    -- `DISPATCH_SPACETIME_SERVER=` exported empty is the shell's way of saying
    -- "unset". Null is not an error any more; it is what selects the managed
    -- store.
    --
    -- configured_store_server() — the address sync.allium's OpenBoardConnection
    -- connects to — is this value when it is non-null, and otherwise the
    -- managed store's config.managed_store_address and
    -- config.managed_database_name once the managed store is ready. The two
    -- names are kept apart because only this one says whether dispatch may
    -- start, publish to, or stop what it connects to.
    explicit_store_server: () -> String?

    -- Whether the `spacetime` CLI is on PATH. Starting and publishing to the
    -- managed store are done through it; stopping is not, because the CLI has
    -- no stop command (see stop_managed_store).
    spacetime_cli_on_path: () -> Boolean

    -- What currently answers on config.managed_store_address.
    probe_managed_address: () -> ManagedAddressState

    -- Start a store listening on config.managed_store_address, detached from
    -- the board's terminal, and wait — at most config.managed_store_start_timeout
    -- — until it answers HTTP as a store. Null on success; otherwise the
    -- failure, whose own message (could not be run, exited, timed out) is
    -- carried into the abort.
    start_managed_store: () -> String?

    -- Whether the managed store holds config.managed_database_name at all. A
    -- store can lose the database while keeping its data directory — cleared
    -- by hand, say — and the recorded hash beside it would then name a module
    -- that is not published anywhere.
    managed_database_exists: () -> Boolean

    -- A hash of the module .wasm embedded in this binary, and the hash the
    -- managed store records for the module last published to
    -- config.managed_database_name (null when none is recorded). The record
    -- is a small file in the managed store's own data directory — outside the
    -- module's schema, so a migration cannot reshape it, readable without a
    -- connection, and independent of the board's --db so that every board on
    -- the managed store reads the same one.
    embedded_module_hash: () -> String
    recorded_module_hash: () -> String?

    -- Publish the embedded module to config.managed_database_name, never with
    -- any option that clears existing data.
    publish_embedded_module: () -> ModulePublishOutcome

    -- Record `hash` in the managed store as the module now published. True
    -- when the record was kept; see ConnectOnceTheModuleIsPublished for why a
    -- false answer does not stop the launch.
    record_module_hash: (hash: String) -> Boolean

    -- Whether this board process has taken on the managed store on this
    -- launch: true from the moment AdoptAStoreAlreadyOnTheManagedAddress
    -- fires or StartTheManagedStoreWhenNothingAnswers attempts a start —
    -- including a start that then failed — and false on every launch that
    -- named a store. It is what decides whether an exit stops the store.
    managed_store_held_by_this_board: () -> Boolean

    -- Stop the store serving config.managed_store_address. Returns only once
    -- it has stopped or config.managed_store_stop_timeout has passed, and
    -- says which. The `spacetime` CLI has no stop command, so this asks the
    -- store process to terminate — the one this board started, and whatever
    -- listens on the managed address (an adopted store) — rather than going
    -- through the CLI.
    stop_managed_store: () -> Boolean

    @invariant StartingDoesNotTieTheStoreToTheTerminal
        -- The started store is not a child of the board's terminal: closing
        -- the pane, detaching tmux or the board's own window being retired
        -- does not deliver it a signal. Its lifetime is ended only by
        -- stop_managed_store, so a store that outlives a board is a store
        -- whose board could not run its exit — see the open question on
        -- uncatchable exits.

    @invariant ReadyMeansServingNotListening
        -- start_managed_store reports success only once the store answers an
        -- HTTP request as a store. A freshly started instance accepts TCP
        -- connections before it serves HTTP, and a publish or a connection
        -- attempted in that gap fails with an error that names the wrong
        -- cause.

    @invariant PublishingNeverClearsData
        -- publish_embedded_module never asks SpacetimeDB to delete data to make
        -- a migration go through. A change the store refuses to migrate
        -- automatically comes back as needs_manual_migration, and what the
        -- operator's database holds is left exactly as it was.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Enumerations
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The configuration artefacts dispatch manages on the operator's behalf. Named
-- individually because the drift report is what the operator reads when they
-- are about to be asked for permission, and "something is out of date" is not
-- enough to consent to.
enum ConfigArtefact {
    mcp_server_entry   -- the dispatch MCP server in Claude Code's user config
    | plugin           -- the embedded skills, commands and hooks
    | status_line      -- the dispatch-owned settings file every spawned
                       -- session is started with. Named for the statusLine
                       -- decorator it was introduced for, but it now carries
                       -- three things: that decorator, the sandbox setting
                       -- (dispatch.allium: SandboxedAgentExecution), and the
                       -- board's own address, which is how the session's
                       -- hooks find it (agent-health.allium: HookDelivery).
                       -- A board on a different address therefore finds this
                       -- artefact out of date, as it must
    | tmux_focus_events -- the focus-events option and its persisted form
    | feed_scripts     -- the shipped feed scripts under <data_dir>/scripts/
}

-- How the drift check ended. Every value is non-fatal: the board draws after
-- all four.
enum StartupConfigOutcome {
    already_current   -- nothing was out of date; nothing was said
    | updated         -- the operator agreed and the artefacts were written
    | declined        -- the operator was asked and said no
    | reported_only   -- no one could be asked, so nothing was written
}

-- Why the command could not put itself inside a tmux session. Both are fatal —
-- unlike configuration drift, the board genuinely cannot run without one.
enum SessionLaunchFailure {
    tmux_unavailable  -- no tmux on PATH
    | launch_rejected -- tmux was reached and refused to start or attach
}

-- The three states retiring the board's window can leave a session in. Named
-- apart because the launch that follows differs in each: one starts the
-- replacement board, one recreates the session, and one aborts.
enum RetireOutcome {
    session_ready          -- the session is there and holds no board window
    | session_discarded    -- the board's window was its last; tmux dropped the session
    | board_window_survived -- the window is still there and could not be closed
}

-- What answers on the managed store's address when the board looks. Three
-- values because the board does three different things: adopts, starts, or
-- aborts.
enum ManagedAddressState {
    store_answering        -- a SpacetimeDB store answers HTTP there
    | nothing_listening    -- nothing accepts a connection there
    | held_by_something_else -- something accepts connections there and is not a store
}

-- How publishing the embedded module to the managed store ended.
enum ModulePublishOutcome {
    published                -- the store accepted it, migrating automatically where needed
    | needs_manual_migration -- the store would only accept it by deleting data
    | publish_failed         -- the publish failed for any other reason
}

-- Every condition that stops the board before it draws. The two session
-- failures above are carried through unchanged; the rest are the ways a
-- launch can find the substrate it needs already taken, unusable, or — in the
-- host's case — unnamed with nobody there to name it, or not readable at all.
enum StartupAbortReason {
    tmux_unavailable              -- no tmux on PATH
    | launch_rejected             -- tmux was reached and refused
    | board_already_in_this_window -- the launch came from the board's own window
    | previous_board_not_retired  -- the old board's window would not close
    | session_unidentified        -- tmux would not say which session this is
    | agent_port_unavailable      -- another process holds the agent port
    | host_unnamed                -- this machine has no label and nobody could be asked for one
    | host_identity_unavailable   -- the store this machine's Host row lives in is unusable: the row could not be read or minted at all, or an unnamed machine's label could not be written back
    | spacetime_cli_missing       -- no store is named, so the board would run its own, and the `spacetime` CLI is not on PATH
    | managed_store_port_taken    -- no store is named, and the managed store's address is held by something that is not a store
    | managed_store_did_not_start -- no store is named, nothing answered on the managed address, and the store started there exited or did not serve HTTP within config.managed_store_start_timeout
    | module_needs_manual_migration -- the embedded module differs from the managed store's, and the store would accept it only by deleting data
    | module_publish_failed       -- the embedded module differs from the managed store's, and publishing it failed for any other reason
    | store_unavailable           -- the first connection to the store failed: unreachable, refused, timed out, not subscribable, or it identified this install as somebody else
}

-- Why there is no longer a store_unconfigured. It was a launch that never said
-- where the store is, and its remedy — a flag or an environment variable, plus
-- two `spacetime` commands run by hand — was a precondition the board can
-- satisfy for itself. A launch that names no store now gets the managed one
-- (BringUpTheManagedStoreOnceTheHostIsNamed), so "unconfigured" is not a
-- failure any more; the reasons that replace it are the ways the managed store
-- itself can fail to come up.
--
-- Why the managed store's failures are five values and not one. Each is
-- repaired by a different act, and the operator reads this enum as a list of
-- distinct things to go and fix. spacetime_cli_missing: install the CLI (the
-- message says how). managed_store_port_taken: stop whatever holds the port,
-- or name a store explicitly. managed_store_did_not_start: read the store's
-- own error, which is attached. module_needs_manual_migration: decide what
-- to do about data the new schema cannot keep, which dispatch will not decide
-- for them (AbortWhenTheModuleCannotMigrateAutomatically). module_publish_failed:
-- read the publish error, which is attached. The two start failures — the
-- process exiting and the wait running out — share one value because both are
-- "the store dispatch started is not serving" and the attached message is what
-- tells them apart, the same reasoning store_unavailable applies below.
--
-- store_unavailable is a store that exists and could not be used, and the
-- remedy is whatever the attached reason says: start the server, fix the
-- address, or resolve the identity conflict sync.allium's
-- StopOnAUserIdentityConflict describes. It covers the managed store too: a
-- managed store that came up and was published to can still fail the first
-- connection, and that failure is the same substrate's. The conflict shares
-- the value rather than earning its own because it is discovered by the same
-- attempt and reported with its own composed message; another value would add
-- a line to the enum without adding a distinction the operator does not
-- already read in the reason.

-- Why host_identity_unavailable is its own value rather than a second way to
-- reach host_unnamed. The two are one question apart — "which machine is this?"
-- — but they are answered at different depths and repaired by different people.
-- host_unnamed means the identity was read successfully and this machine has no
-- label; the remedy is a human typing one, and the message says so ("run
-- `dispatch tui` interactively once to name it"). It does NOT guarantee the
-- store would go on to accept that label — nothing on the non-interactive path
-- ever attempts the write, so a store that would in fact refuse it is
-- indistinguishable from one that would not until an operator actually answers
-- (AbortWhenTheHostIsUnnamedAndNoOneCanAnswer's guidance names this gap
-- explicitly). host_identity_unavailable means the store itself is unusable —
-- the read or the mint failed outright, or an unnamed machine's label was
-- handed over and would not persist — so no answer a human could type would
-- help; the remedy is whatever is wrong with the store the identity lives in.
--
-- There is deliberately ONE value for both of the store's failure directions
-- rather than a second alongside it (a host_label_not_persisted, say). The two
-- reach the board by different routes — one at the gate
-- (AbortWhenTheHostIdentityStoreIsUnusable), one when the operator's answer is
-- handed to host.allium's RenameHost (startup.allium: NameHostFromStartupPrompt)
-- — but they are the same broken substrate and they are repaired by the same
-- act, so a second value would carry word-for-word the remedy this one already
-- carries. This enum is read as a list of distinct things to go and fix; a
-- value that adds a route rather than a remedy makes the list longer without
-- making it more useful.

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Config
------------------------------------------------------------

config {
    -- The tmux session `dispatch tui` creates for itself when run outside one.
    -- Fixed rather than derived: it is the name the operator reattaches to by
    -- hand, and a name that varies per invocation cannot be reattached to.
    session_name: String = "dispatch"

    -- The name the board's own tmux window carries. The board's window is
    -- given this name as it is created, and this is how a later launch finds
    -- the window to retire. One name serves both, so a launch cannot look for
    -- a window under a name the board never adopts.
    --
    -- Unique **within a session**, not across the tmux server — the one
    -- exception to dispatch.allium's `TmuxWindowNamesAreUnique`, which that
    -- invariant names. Two sessions each running their own board is a state
    -- the operator can reach on purpose, and a server-wide rule would let a
    -- window in a session dispatch does not own refuse the replacement board
    -- on the restart path, after the previous one had already been retired.
    board_window_name: String = "TUI"

    -- Where the managed store listens, and the database dispatch's module is
    -- published to there. Loopback only: the managed store is this machine's,
    -- and nothing about running it should expose it to the network. Fixed
    -- rather than chosen per launch, because a later launch and the agents'
    -- reconnects must find the same store the previous board left data in.
    managed_store_address: String = "127.0.0.1:3000"
    managed_database_name: String = "dispatch"

    -- How long a started managed store has to serve HTTP before the launch
    -- gives up on it, and how long stopping it may hold up the board's exit.
    managed_store_start_timeout: Duration = 15.seconds
    managed_store_stop_timeout: Duration = 5.seconds
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules
------------------------------------------------------------

rule DrawInTheWindowThisBoardWasStartedIn {
    when: LaunchBoard(operator)
    requires: inside_tmux_session and launching_as_the_started_board

    ensures: CheckStartupConfig(operator)

    @guidance
        -- This process is the board, running in the window a launch created for
        -- it moments ago. That launch has already retired whatever board came
        -- before, so there is nothing left to retire — and retiring now would
        -- close this very window, taking this board down before it drew and,
        -- where the window was the session's only one, the session with it.
        --
        -- Every board reaches this rule: both entry paths create the board's
        -- window and hand it the invocation, so the process that comes up runs
        -- the launch path like any other. It is the ordinary case, not an edge.
}

rule LaunchBoardInsideExistingSession {
    when: LaunchBoard(operator)
    requires: inside_tmux_session
              and session_is_identified
              and not launching_from_the_board_window
              and not launching_as_the_started_board

    ensures: RetireBoardWindow(operator, session: current_session)

    @guidance
        -- The operator already had a session and ran the command in a window of
        -- their own, so the board draws there and is a sibling of whatever else
        -- they had open.
        --
        -- Any board already open in that session is retired first, even though
        -- this process is about to draw in a different window. The rule is the
        -- same one the outside-tmux path follows, and for the same reason: a
        -- second board beside the first would bind the same agent port and
        -- fight over the same tmux keybindings.
        --
        -- The window this process is in is never the one retired. That is what
        -- the guard above buys: without it, a board drawing in a window already
        -- carrying config.board_window_name retires itself.
}

rule ContinueAfterRetiringOnTheInsideTmuxPath {
    when: BoardWindowRetired(operator, session, outcome)
    requires: outcome != board_window_survived and inside_tmux_session

    ensures: CheckStartupConfig(operator)

    @guidance
        -- This process already has its window, so there is nothing to enter and
        -- nothing to attach to: it carries straight on to the configuration
        -- check and then draws. Both non-fatal retire outcomes arrive here.
        -- `session_discarded` cannot occur on this path — a session whose only
        -- window was the board's has no other window for this process to be
        -- running in — but the rule does not need to say which of the two it
        -- got, and stating it as "anything that did not abort" keeps it from
        -- depending on that argument staying true.
}

rule RefuseToLaunchFromInsideTheBoardWindow {
    when: LaunchBoard(operator)
    requires: inside_tmux_session and launching_from_the_board_window

    ensures: StartupAborted(reason: board_already_in_this_window)

    @guidance
        -- The board's window can hold a second pane — a pinned agent, split
        -- beside the board — and the operator can type into it. Retiring the
        -- board's window from a shell inside that window would close the shell
        -- issuing the command, so this launch refuses instead. It is the one
        -- place where the restart rule would destroy its own caller, and the
        -- operator's next move is obvious once they are told: run it from
        -- another window, or from outside tmux.
        --
        -- `launching_from_the_board_window` fails safe towards "no": a probe
        -- that cannot be answered lets the launch proceed rather than refusing
        -- it. Refusing on a failed probe would block the operator over a tmux
        -- hiccup and leave them nothing to do about it, while proceeding risks
        -- only the narrow case this rule covers. A tmux that cannot answer
        -- `#{window_name}` is one whose next command is unlikely to succeed
        -- either, so the failure surfaces a step later regardless.
}

rule RefuseWhenTheSessionCannotBeIdentified {
    when: LaunchBoard(operator)
    requires: inside_tmux_session and not session_is_identified

    ensures: StartupAborted(reason: session_unidentified)

    @guidance
        -- Inside a session tmux will not name, there is no session to scope the
        -- retire to. Guessing is the one thing that must not happen here: an
        -- unscoped retire closes a window in whatever session tmux resolves the
        -- empty target to, and that may be one dispatch does not own.
        --
        -- Reported as its own reason rather than folded into
        -- previous_board_not_retired. They call for opposite actions — one
        -- tells the operator to close a specific window, the other says tmux
        -- is not answering — and a launch that names the wrong session in its
        -- remedy sends them to close a window that may not exist.
}

rule LaunchBoardBySupplyingASession {
    when: LaunchBoard(operator)
    requires: not inside_tmux_session
              and not session_exists(config.session_name)

    ensures: EnterSession(session: config.session_name)

    @guidance
        -- The cold path: no session of dispatch's own yet. The command puts
        -- itself inside one rather than reporting that it is not in one. It
        -- hands its own arguments — the database path and the agent port — to
        -- the session it enters, so the board that comes up inside tmux is the
        -- board the operator asked for and not a default one.
        --
        -- This process does not survive the handoff, and deliberately: the
        -- operator's shell waits on the tmux client, so detaching returns them
        -- to their prompt with nothing orphaned behind it.
        --
        -- The session's first window is given `config.board_window_name` as
        -- it is created, exactly as the restart path names the window it adds.
        -- A board that dies before it does anything still leaves a window the
        -- next launch can find and retire, and a board that never adopted the
        -- name is a board a later launch starts a rival beside.
        --
        -- Implementation: `tmux new-session -A -s <session_name>` re-invoking
        -- this binary, via exec rather than a child process. `-A` stays for
        -- `NeverCreatesASecondSession`: between reading session_exists and
        -- reaching here, a session could have appeared, and create-or-attach
        -- is the one form that cannot produce a rival.
}

rule RestartTheBoardInTheExistingSession {
    when: LaunchBoard(operator)
    requires: not inside_tmux_session
              and session_exists(config.session_name)

    ensures: RetireBoardWindow(operator, session: config.session_name)

    @guidance
        -- The common case, and the one this rule exists to change: dispatch's
        -- session is still there from last time, with agent windows in it and
        -- a board window holding whatever the last run left behind. The board
        -- window goes, a fresh board starts in the same session, and the
        -- operator lands on it.
        --
        -- No liveness check precedes this. A board that is running is retired
        -- exactly like one that has exited, because the operator typed the
        -- command that starts a board — usually because the running one is
        -- built from a binary they have since replaced, which no liveness
        -- check would notice.
}

rule RetireTheBoardWindow {
    when: RetireBoardWindow(operator, session)

    let outcome = retire_board_window(session)

    ensures: BoardWindowRetired(operator, session, outcome)

    @guidance
        -- One rule rather than one per starting state, because retiring an
        -- absent board window and retiring a present one leave the session in
        -- the same place — `session_ready`. Splitting them would invite an
        -- error report for the case where there was nothing to do.
        --
        -- The agent windows beside it are untouched
        -- (`RetiringTouchesOnlyTheBoardWindow`). If the board's window was the
        -- session's only one, tmux discards the session along with it, and the
        -- outcome is how the launch path finds out.
        --
        -- The outcome is read back from tmux rather than inferred from whether
        -- the close was reported as working. `AFailedRetireIsNotMistakenForSuccess`
        -- — what matters is the state the session is in now, and asking is the
        -- only way to know it.
}

rule AbortWhenThePreviousBoardWillNotClose {
    when: BoardWindowRetired(operator, session, outcome)
    requires: outcome = board_window_survived

    ensures: StartupAborted(reason: previous_board_not_retired)

    @guidance
        -- The one retire outcome that stops the launch. Carrying on would start
        -- a second board beside a live one, which is the state
        -- `ExactlyOneBoardPerSession` exists to prevent — and the operator would
        -- meet it as the agent port already being taken, a message that names
        -- nothing about what actually went wrong.
        --
        -- This is reported rather than retried. A window that will not close
        -- is not a race the launch can wait out; something the operator has to
        -- look at is holding it.
}

rule StartFreshBoardInSurvivingSession {
    when: BoardWindowRetired(operator, session, outcome)
    requires: outcome = session_ready and not inside_tmux_session

    ensures: SessionAttached(session)

    @guidance
        -- The session outlived its board window because agent windows remain.
        -- Plain attaching would put the operator inside a session with no
        -- board in it, so the session is given a new window running this
        -- invocation's argv first, and only then attached to.
        --
        -- Implementation: `tmux new-window -t <session> -n <board_window_name>
        -- -- <argv>` followed by an exec'd `tmux attach`. The window is named
        -- up front rather than left for the board to rename, so a board that
        -- dies before it renames anything still leaves a window the next
        -- launch can find and retire.
}

rule ReportFreshBoardFailure {
    when: BoardWindowRetired(operator, session, outcome)
    requires: outcome = session_ready
              and not inside_tmux_session
              and not can_start_fresh_board(session)

    ensures: StartupAborted(reason: launch_rejected)

    @guidance
        -- The previous board has already been retired by the time this can
        -- fire, so the operator is left with neither board. That is the cost
        -- AbortWhenThePreviousBoardWillNotClose exists to avoid paying, and it
        -- is paid here anyway — there is no way to undo a retire, and the
        -- alternative is worse in both directions. Falling back to
        -- `EnterSession` would attach to the session without running the board
        -- at all, since tmux ignores argv for a session that exists; leaving
        -- the operator attached to a boardless session says nothing went wrong
        -- when something did.
        --
        -- So it aborts, and the operator's shell is where they read why. The
        -- session and its agent windows are untouched and a second
        -- `dispatch tui` is the retry — the retire it repeats is a no-op now.
}

rule RecreateSessionRetiredWithItsLastWindow {
    when: BoardWindowRetired(operator, session, outcome)
    requires: outcome = session_discarded and not inside_tmux_session

    ensures: EnterSession(session)

    @guidance
        -- The board's window was the session's only one, so tmux discarded the
        -- session when it closed. There is nothing left to attach to and the
        -- launch falls back to the cold path, which creates the session and
        -- runs the board in it. The operator sees no difference: they asked for
        -- a board and they get one.
}

rule ReportSessionLaunchFailure {
    when: EnterSession(session)
    requires: not can_enter_session(session)

    ensures: StartupAborted(reason: session_failure_reason(session))

    @guidance
        -- The board cannot draw without a session, so there is nothing to
        -- degrade to — unlike a configuration artefact, which the operator can
        -- fix later. The report names which of the two failures occurred, so a
        -- missing tmux is not mistaken for a broken one.
}

rule CheckStartupConfigWhenNothingIsStale {
    when: CheckStartupConfig(operator)
    let drift = inspect_config_drift()
    requires: drift.is_clean

    ensures: StartupConfigResolved(operator, outcome: already_current)

    @guidance
        -- The steady state, and it says nothing at all. A line reporting that
        -- four artefacts are current on every single launch trains the operator
        -- to skip the region of the screen where the one line that matters
        -- appears.
}

rule PromptToUpdateStaleConfig {
    when: CheckStartupConfig(operator)
    let drift = inspect_config_drift()
    requires: not drift.is_clean and operator_can_answer

    ensures: ConfigUpdatePrompted(operator, drift)

    @guidance
        -- One prompt for the whole report, not one per artefact. The artefacts
        -- are listed in it so the operator knows what they are agreeing to, but
        -- they are not separable decisions — a half-updated installation is a
        -- configuration nobody chose and nothing tests.
}

rule ApplyStaleConfigOnConsent {
    when: ConfigUpdateAccepted(operator, drift)

    let failed = apply_config_update(drift)

    ensures:
        StartupConfigResolved(operator, outcome: updated)

    @guidance
        -- The one and only path in this spec that writes to the operator's
        -- configuration directory.
        --
        -- The outcome is `updated` whether or not every artefact could be
        -- written: an artefact that failed is named to the operator and left
        -- for the next launch to retry, because the alternative — refusing the
        -- whole update over one unwritable file — leaves the operator with
        -- neither the update nor a way to make progress.
}

rule ContinueAfterDeclinedConfigUpdate {
    when: ConfigUpdateDeclined(operator, drift)

    ensures: StartupConfigResolved(operator, outcome: declined)

    @guidance
        -- Declining is not an error and does not stop the board. The operator
        -- keeps a stale installation on purpose, is told once that they have
        -- one, and is asked again next launch.
}

rule ReportStaleConfigWhenNoOneCanAnswer {
    when: CheckStartupConfig(operator)
    let drift = inspect_config_drift()
    requires: not drift.is_clean and not operator_can_answer

    ensures: StartupConfigResolved(operator, outcome: reported_only)

    @guidance
        -- A non-interactive invocation — a script, a CI job, input redirected
        -- from nowhere — cannot consent, so it is told what is stale and
        -- nothing is written. Treating silence as agreement is how a scripted
        -- run ends up modifying the home directory of whoever happened to run
        -- it.
}

rule CheckHostLabelAfterStartupConfigResolves {
    when: StartupConfigResolved(operator, outcome)
    requires: agent_port_available

    ensures: CheckHostLabel(operator)

    @guidance
        -- Every configuration outcome reaches here. The configuration check is
        -- a convenience placed in front of the board, never a gate in front of
        -- it; what follows it IS a gate, and the two are deliberately adjacent
        -- so the difference is read in one place.
        --
        -- It runs after the port claim rather than before it. A launch that is
        -- going to abort on a port somebody else holds must not first ask the
        -- operator to name their machine: the answer would be taken and then
        -- thrown away with the launch, and the next launch would ask again.
        --
        -- It runs after the configuration prompt rather than before it for the
        -- same reason in reverse: both questions are answered on the ordinary
        -- terminal before the board takes the screen, and asking the rarest one
        -- last means the machine is named once, on the launch that mints it,
        -- rather than in front of a drift report the operator is still reading.
}

rule ContinueWhenTheHostIsAlreadyNamed {
    when: CheckHostLabel(operator)
    requires: core/local_host() != null and core/local_host().label != null

    ensures: HostLabelResolved(operator)

    @guidance
        -- The steady state, and like the clean drift report it says nothing at
        -- all. A machine is named once; every launch after that passes straight
        -- through.
}

rule PromptForHostLabelWhenUnnamed {
    when: CheckHostLabel(operator)
    requires: core/local_host() != null
              and core/local_host().label = null
              and operator_can_answer

    ensures: HostLabelPrompted(operator, suggested: machine_hostname())

    @guidance
        -- The first launch after minting, and normally the only one that ever
        -- asks. The machine's hostname is offered pre-filled, so the operator's
        -- cheapest answer is the sensible default rather than an empty field:
        -- the hostname keeps the job it had when minting seeded the label
        -- silently (host.allium: MintHostIdentity), and loses only the silence.
        --
        -- THIS PROMPT CANNOT BE DECLINED, and that is the one place it departs
        -- from the configuration prompt it otherwise mirrors.
        -- ContinueAfterDeclinedConfigUpdate exists because a stale plugin tree
        -- costs the operator nothing the board needs; there is no counterpart
        -- here, and adding one would be adding a way to reach a drawn board
        -- with an unnamed machine.
        --
        -- Why that matters now rather than in the abstract: Task.host is about
        -- to become a value other people read (see the ownership model in
        -- docs/superpowers/specs/2026-09-13-distributed-dispatch-design.md).
        -- "Some unnamed machine holds this worktree" is not something a
        -- teammate can act on, and the moment to fix it is before the first
        -- task is stamped with this host — not after a shared board has already
        -- shown the gap to somebody else.
        --
        -- This rule does not know in advance whether the store will accept the
        -- answer it is about to collect — that is only discoverable by
        -- attempting the write, and nothing here attempts one. A store that
        -- will in fact refuse the label is still prompted; the failure surfaces
        -- one step later, when NameHostFromStartupPrompt hands the answer to
        -- host.allium's RenameHost and the write does not take — aborting with
        -- the same `host_identity_unavailable` reason
        -- AbortWhenTheHostIdentityStoreIsUnusable uses for the read/mint
        -- failure, ensured by NameHostFromStartupPrompt itself rather than by
        -- that rule's own guard (see its guidance). Gating the prompt itself
        -- on writability would need a probe nobody has built and a signal
        -- (`core/Host` carries no "last write failed" field) that has nowhere
        -- to live.
}

rule NameHostFromStartupPrompt {
    when: HostLabelSubmitted(operator, label)
    requires: trim(label) != ""

    ensures:
        RenameHost(label: label)
        if core/local_host().label != null:
            HostLabelResolved(operator)
        else:
            StartupAborted(reason: host_identity_unavailable)

    @guidance
        -- This rule does not write the label. It hands the operator's answer to
        -- host.allium's RenameHost, which is the single rule that writes it, and
        -- then lets the launch continue. Assigning the label here instead would
        -- put the trim and the empty-label refusal in two files that can
        -- disagree, and the disagreement would be silent in the direction that
        -- matters: a startup path accepting what a later rename refuses.
        --
        -- The requires clause is the same predicate RenameHost applies, stated
        -- here so a submission that cannot be honoured is rejected at the
        -- prompt and the prompt stays up, rather than resolving the gate
        -- against a write that did not happen.
        --
        -- IT RESOLVES THE GATE ONLY IF THE LABEL IS ACTUALLY THERE AFTERWARDS.
        -- The branch reads the state RenameHost leaves behind, so the two
        -- outcomes of handing the answer over are told apart without this rule
        -- taking over the write: a label that persisted resolves the gate, and
        -- a persist that did not take aborts instead of reporting success —
        -- with the same `host_identity_unavailable` reason
        -- AbortWhenTheHostIdentityStoreIsUnusable uses for a read/mint that
        -- failed outright, rather than as an unclassified error propagated out
        -- of the launch.
        --
        -- THIS RULE, NOT A WIDENED CheckHostLabel GUARD, IS WHERE THE PERSIST
        -- FAILURE IS CAUGHT. The two failures are the same broken settings
        -- store and share one `StartupAbortReason`
        -- (AbortWhenTheHostIdentityStoreIsUnusable's guidance records why one
        -- reason, not two) — but they are ensured by two different rules
        -- rather than one rule whose guard covers both. A single
        -- `CheckHostLabel(operator)`-triggered rule cannot tell "this is the
        -- first time the machine has been asked" apart from "this is a
        -- retry after RenameHost's write just failed": both look identical —
        -- `core/local_host().label = null` — and `core/Host` carries no field
        -- recording that a write was attempted and failed. Manufacturing one
        -- (an ambient "is the store writable" predicate, tried and reverted)
        -- read as though something in the system could know in advance
        -- whether a write will succeed, which nothing does — the only way to
        -- find out is to attempt it, which is exactly what this rule's `when`
        -- already does. Catching the failure where the write is attempted
        -- needs no such predicate and asserts nothing unverifiable.
}

rule AbortWhenTheHostIsUnnamedAndNoOneCanAnswer {
    when: CheckHostLabel(operator)
    requires: core/local_host() != null
              and core/local_host().label = null
              and not operator_can_answer

    ensures: StartupAborted(reason: host_unnamed)

    @guidance
        -- The non-interactive first launch: a script, a CI job, stdin redirected
        -- from nowhere. It aborts, and this is the one place where this spec's
        -- usual answer — report it and step over it, as
        -- ReportStaleConfigWhenNoOneCanAnswer does — is the wrong one.
        --
        -- The two questions differ in what silence costs. A stale artefact that
        -- goes unwritten leaves the operator exactly as they were, and the next
        -- interactive launch asks again. An unnamed host that goes unasked does
        -- not stay still: the board draws, tasks are dispatched, and every one
        -- of them is stamped with a host nobody can name. Stepping over the
        -- question would produce precisely the state the gate exists to
        -- prevent, and produce it durably.
        --
        -- So it fails where the operator can read it, exactly like a missing
        -- tmux: this is a substrate the board needs and cannot obtain for
        -- itself. Hostname-as-silent-default is the tempting alternative and is
        -- rejected — it is the behaviour decision 1 removed, and reinstating it
        -- on the one path where nobody is watching is where it would do the
        -- most damage.
        --
        -- The remedy is one interactive `dispatch tui`, which asks once and
        -- unblocks every scripted launch afterwards.
        --
        -- This rule speaks only for a host that was read. The guard says so
        -- explicitly, because "no label" and "no answer about the label at
        -- all" would otherwise arrive here together and be reported with a
        -- remedy that only fits the first
        -- (AbortWhenTheHostIdentityStoreIsUnusable).
        --
        -- IT DOES NOT DISTINGUISH A STORE THAT WOULD REFUSE THE LABEL ANYWAY.
        -- A non-interactive launch against a machine whose settings store
        -- cannot actually accept a write still reports `host_unnamed`, with a
        -- remedy ("run interactively") that will not fix the underlying store
        -- problem — it will only get the operator as far as answering the
        -- prompt, at which point NameHostFromStartupPrompt's own attempt to
        -- persist the answer is what actually discovers the store is broken,
        -- and reports `host_identity_unavailable` on that later launch. This
        -- is accepted rather than pre-empted: nothing on the non-interactive
        -- path ever attempts a write, so nothing here could know the store
        -- would have refused one, and no field on `core/Host` records that
        -- fact for this rule to read instead.
}

rule AbortWhenTheHostIdentityStoreIsUnusable {
    when: CheckHostLabel(operator)
    requires: core/local_host() = null

    ensures: StartupAborted(reason: host_identity_unavailable)

    @guidance
        -- The gate's fourth arm: this install could not read its Host row and
        -- could not mint one (host.allium: MintHostIdentity). There is no id
        -- and no label, so the board cannot say which machine it is by any
        -- route at all — the first of two faces of one broken settings store,
        -- and the one this rule's own guard covers.
        --
        -- THE SECOND FACE — the identity read fine, the machine is unnamed, and
        -- the label the operator just supplied could not be written back — is
        -- covered by the SAME `host_identity_unavailable` reason but is NOT a
        -- second arm of this rule's guard. It is ensured directly by
        -- startup.allium's NameHostFromStartupPrompt, when host.allium's
        -- RenameHost fails; see that rule's guidance for why the split is a
        -- rule per trigger rather than one rule with a widened guard. Both
        -- faces are the same substrate refusing to do its job in one direction
        -- or the other, which is why the rule is named for the STORE rather
        -- than for an identity that "cannot be determined": on the second face
        -- the identity WAS determined, and the thing that failed is the same
        -- substrate as the first face's outright read/mint failure.
        --
        -- A store that cannot be written but can be read, on a machine that
        -- was already named, needs no write at all — it passes through
        -- ContinueWhenTheHostIsAlreadyNamed untouched by either face. Only an
        -- unnamed machine ever reaches a write attempt, so only an unnamed
        -- machine's launch can hit the second face.
        --
        -- IT ABORTS RATHER THAN DEGRADING, and the argument is not "be strict"
        -- — tolerating it does not degrade, it corrupts. The claim that
        -- precedes a dispatch applies core.allium's `is_locally_owned`, which
        -- has two arms: nobody holds this task, or this machine does. An
        -- identity that could not be determined fails the second arm against
        -- every task — but it does not touch the FIRST, and the first is the
        -- one that matches every task that has never been dispatched. So THE
        -- CLAIM STILL SUCCEEDS on exactly the tasks a first dispatch acts on.
        -- Dispatch then goes on to provision a worktree, while the step that
        -- stamps the owning host onto the task skips itself for want of an id
        -- to stamp (dispatch.allium: DispatchTask's best-effort host stamp).
        -- What is left behind is a task row with a worktree and no host — a
        -- direct, silent violation of
        -- core.allium's HostTracksWorktree, written to the database and
        -- surviving every later launch, including the ones where the identity
        -- reads fine again. Every locality gate built on
        -- Task.is_locally_owned then reads those rows as unowned and lets any
        -- machine act on a worktree that is not its own.
        --
        -- The second half of the argument is that there is nothing worth
        -- saving by carrying on. The store the host identity lives in is the
        -- one the board reads constantly; a board that cannot answer a single
        -- settings read at startup is not about to become useful a frame
        -- later. Failing here, on the operator's terminal, with the read
        -- error attached, costs a launch that was going to fail anyway and
        -- buys back the corruption above.
        --
        -- The remedy is not the one host_unnamed offers. Running interactively
        -- and typing a name changes nothing — on the first face nothing is
        -- asking for a name, and on the second the name would be asked for and
        -- then dropped. Either way the operator has to repair whatever is wrong
        -- with the database or its directory. That difference is why this is
        -- its own abort reason (see the note under StartupAbortReason), and the
        -- identical remedy across both faces is why it is ONE reason rather
        -- than two: a second value would carry the same sentence, and a reason
        -- that cannot say anything the first does not has not earned a place in
        -- an enum the operator reads as a list of distinct things to go and fix.
        -- The message's core clause — "could not reach or write its settings"
        -- — already names the remedy for both faces, even though its framing
        -- leans toward the first ("identity could not be read or created");
        -- that lean is accepted rather than reworded, since the remedy is what
        -- the operator acts on and it is identical either way.
        --
        -- It does not matter whether anybody can answer. Unlike the rule
        -- above, this one has no operator_can_answer arm: an interactive
        -- launch and a scripted one are equally stuck, and there is no
        -- question worth putting to the human who is there.
}

rule AbortWhenTheManagedStoreHasNoCli {
    when: LaunchBoard(operator)
    requires: explicit_store_server() = null and not spacetime_cli_on_path()

    ensures: StartupAborted(reason: spacetime_cli_missing)

    @guidance
        -- THE SHARED STORE IS MANDATORY (Phase 12 of the SpacetimeDB migration,
        -- task #4916), and a launch that names none gets the managed one
        -- (BringUpTheManagedStoreOnceTheHostIsNamed). Running it needs the
        -- `spacetime` CLI — to start the store and publish to it —
        -- so a launch without one cannot succeed, and says so with an install
        -- hint rather than failing later with a subprocess error.
        --
        -- CHECKED FIRST, on the operator's own terminal, before a tmux session
        -- is supplied or anything else is asked — the position the retired
        -- store_unconfigured check held, for the same reason. It needs nothing
        -- but the command line, the environment and PATH, and every later step
        -- would be work done for a launch that cannot succeed. The started
        -- board repeats it, harmlessly: LaunchBoard is every process's first
        -- step.
        --
        -- A launch that names a store does not need the CLI and is not asked
        -- for it (ANamedStoreIsNeverManaged).
}

rule ConnectToTheNamedStoreOnceTheHostIsNamed {
    when: HostLabelResolved(operator)
    requires: explicit_store_server() != null

    ensures: StoreConnectionAttemptedAtStartup(operator)

    @guidance
        -- A STORE THE OPERATOR NAMED IS CONNECTED TO AS IT IS. Dispatch starts
        -- nothing, publishes nothing and will stop nothing there: the address
        -- may be a colleague's server, a shared team store, or a loopback one
        -- the operator runs by hand on purpose, and none of those is dispatch's
        -- to manage. A module there older or newer than this binary expects is
        -- reported through the first connection's failure (store_unavailable,
        -- with the reason attached), never fixed by publishing over it.
        --

        -- THE FIRST CONNECTION IS PART OF STARTING, and it finishes before the
        -- board draws — sync.allium: OpenBoardConnection. It comes after the
        -- host identity because the subscription is scoped by host id
        -- (settings are this host's), and before BoardDrawn because
        -- everything the board reads while it starts — repo paths, settings,
        -- the example feed epic it seeds — is a
        -- shared row, and a board that read them before the store answered
        -- would read nothing.
        --
        -- ONLY THE FIRST ATTEMPT. Once the board has drawn, a drop is an
        -- ordinary outage: sync.allium's ConnectionDropped and
        -- RetryAfterBackoff govern it and nothing here does.
}

rule BringUpTheManagedStoreOnceTheHostIsNamed {
    when: HostLabelResolved(operator)
    requires: explicit_store_server() = null

    ensures: ManagedStoreRequested(operator)

    @guidance
        -- NO STORE NAMED MEANS DISPATCH'S OWN, on config.managed_store_address
        -- with its module in config.managed_database_name. The operator used to
        -- have to run `spacetime start` and `spacetime publish` by hand and
        -- then name the result; both are preconditions the board can satisfy
        -- for itself, and the address is loopback, so nobody else's server is
        -- touched. It is provided silently, like the tmux session and for the
        -- same reason: it is dispatch's own, and it writes nothing into the
        -- operator's configuration (ConfigurationIsNeverWrittenWithoutConsent
        -- is untouched — the store's data directory is the store's).
        --
        -- IT HAPPENS HERE, at the point the first connection would otherwise
        -- be made, rather than first thing. The process that draws the board
        -- is the one that brings the store up, because it is the one whose exit
        -- stops it (StopTheManagedStoreWhenTheBoardExits); a launch outside
        -- tmux hands off to that process and is gone. And every gate before
        -- this one — the port claim, the configuration prompt, the host label
        -- — can abort or wait on a human, so a store started earlier would be
        -- started for launches that then go nowhere. Only the CLI check runs
        -- earlier (AbortWhenTheManagedStoreHasNoCli), because it costs nothing.
}

rule AdoptAStoreAlreadyOnTheManagedAddress {
    when: ManagedStoreRequested(operator)
    let state = probe_managed_address()
    requires: state = store_answering

    ensures: ManagedStoreReady(operator)

    @guidance
        -- A store already serving the managed address is used rather than a
        -- second one started: there cannot be two on one address, and the
        -- one there is holding the data a previous board left. It is most
        -- often the store a board that could not run its exit left behind,
        -- and sometimes one the operator started by hand.
        --
        -- ADOPTING IT MAKES IT THIS BOARD'S. From here managed_store_held_by_this_board()
        -- is true, the module check that follows may publish to it, and this
        -- board's exit stops it — exactly as though this board had started
        -- it. One rule for every managed store, whoever started it, was the
        -- operator's decision: a store that sometimes survives the board and
        -- sometimes does not, depending on history the operator cannot see, is
        -- worse than one that always goes. The operator who wants a store that
        -- outlives the board names it explicitly (ANamedStoreIsNeverManaged).
}

rule ImportBringsUpTheManagedStore {
    when: StoreImportRequested(operator, target)

    ensures:
        if target = null:
            ManagedStoreRequested(operator)
        else:
            ConnectToTheNamedStore(operator, target)

    @guidance
        -- `dispatch store import --from <old store>` (spacetime-seed.allium:
        -- ImportOldStore) writes into the store a board would run, so it
        -- reaches that store the way a board does. With no store named it
        -- adopts or starts the managed store and publishes the embedded
        -- module (the rules above), and because it then holds the store
        -- (managed_store_held_by_this_board) it STOPS it again when the
        -- import ends, success or refusal — leaving the machine as it found
        -- it, so the next `dispatch tui` starts the store itself exactly as
        -- usual. A store the operator named is written to as it is and never
        -- stopped (ANamedStoreIsNeverManaged).
        --
        -- IT DOES NOT REQUIRE A HOST LABEL OR A BOARD: no window, port claim,
        -- configuration prompt or host-name prompt. The import has no use for
        -- them and the operator is typically running it in a plain shell.
}

rule AbortWhenTheManagedAddressIsHeldByAnotherProgram {
    when: ManagedStoreRequested(operator)
    let state = probe_managed_address()
    requires: state = held_by_something_else

    ensures: StartupAborted(reason: managed_store_port_taken)

    @guidance
        -- Something that is not a store holds the port. Starting a store would
        -- fail to bind, and connecting would talk to a program that is not
        -- one, so the launch stops and names the address. The remedy is the
        -- operator's: stop that program, or name a store explicitly.
        --
        -- Nothing was started or adopted, so there is nothing to stop — and the
        -- program on the port is certainly not dispatch's to stop.
}

rule StartTheManagedStoreWhenNothingAnswers {
    when: ManagedStoreRequested(operator)
    let state = probe_managed_address()
    requires: state = nothing_listening

    let failure = start_managed_store()

    ensures:
        if failure = null:
            ManagedStoreReady(operator)
        else:
            StartupAborted(reason: managed_store_did_not_start)

    @guidance
        -- The first launch on a machine, and every launch after a board that
        -- stopped its store on exit. The store is started detached from the
        -- board's terminal (StartingDoesNotTieTheStoreToTheTerminal) on
        -- loopback, and the launch waits until it serves HTTP
        -- (ReadyMeansServingNotListening), bounded by
        -- config.managed_store_start_timeout. The data it serves is whatever a
        -- previous managed store left on disk; starting one never resets it.
        --
        -- managed_store_held_by_this_board() is true from the moment the start
        -- is attempted, not from the moment it succeeds, so a store that was
        -- launched and then missed the deadline is still stopped when the
        -- launch aborts (StopTheManagedStoreWhenStartupAborts). Otherwise a
        -- slow store would come up a moment after the abort, detached and
        -- unowned.
        --
        -- NO RETRY. As with the first connection, a store that did not come up
        -- is better answered now, with its own error attached, than behind a
        -- spinner.
}

rule ConnectWhenTheManagedModuleIsCurrent {
    when: ManagedStoreReady(operator)
    requires:
        recorded_module_hash() = embedded_module_hash()
        managed_database_exists()

    ensures: StoreConnectionAttemptedAtStartup(operator)

    @guidance
        -- The steady state: the store already runs the module this binary
        -- embeds, so nothing is published. The database is asked about only
        -- here, where the hash would otherwise say "current": a record that
        -- survived the database being cleared must not stand in for it. Publishing unconditionally would be
        -- correct but costs a module upload and a migration check on every
        -- launch, for nothing.
        --
        -- THE HASH IS OF THE EMBEDDED .wasm, NOT OF THE SOURCE. The binary
        -- carries a committed, prebuilt module (CI fails when its source stamp
        -- is stale against spacetime/module, as it does for the committed
        -- client bindings), so the thing compared is exactly the thing that
        -- would be published. The recorded hash lives in the managed store's
        -- own data directory, not the board's --db directory, so every board
        -- on the managed store reads the same record — a second binary
        -- pointed at the same store, an older worktree build say, sees a
        -- difference and publishes its own — and a throwaway --db cannot
        -- desync it.
}

rule PublishTheEmbeddedModuleWhenTheStoreDiffers {
    when: ManagedStoreReady(operator)
    requires:
        recorded_module_hash() != embedded_module_hash()
            or not managed_database_exists()

    ensures: ModulePublishFinished(operator, outcome: publish_embedded_module())

    @guidance
        -- A recorded hash that differs, a store that has recorded none — a
        -- fresh store, or one whose module was published by hand — and a
        -- matching record beside a store that no longer holds the database
        -- are the same case: the store cannot be shown to run this binary's
        -- module, so the embedded one is published to
        -- config.managed_database_name.
        --
        -- BEFORE THE FIRST CONNECTION, because the connection subscribes to
        -- the module's tables and calls its reducers: a board that connected
        -- first would subscribe against the schema it is about to replace.
        --
        -- A publish to an older binary's store can go backwards as well as
        -- forwards. It is treated the same way — SpacetimeDB decides whether it
        -- migrates automatically, and a change it refuses reaches
        -- AbortWhenTheModuleCannotMigrateAutomatically like any other.
}

rule ConnectOnceTheModuleIsPublished {
    when: ModulePublishFinished(operator, outcome)
    requires: outcome = published

    ensures:
        recorded_module_hash() = embedded_module_hash()
        StoreConnectionAttemptedAtStartup(operator)

    @guidance
        -- The hash is recorded only after the store accepted the module, so a
        -- recorded hash always names a module the store actually runs. If
        -- recording itself fails the launch still connects: the store is
        -- current, and the only cost is that the next launch publishes the
        -- same module again and finds nothing to migrate.
}

rule AbortWhenTheModuleCannotMigrateAutomatically {
    when: ModulePublishFinished(operator, outcome)
    requires: outcome = needs_manual_migration

    ensures: StartupAborted(reason: module_needs_manual_migration)

    @guidance
        -- THE OPERATOR'S DATA IS NEVER CLEARED TO MAKE A PUBLISH GO THROUGH
        -- (PublishingNeverClearsData). The store has said this schema change
        -- cannot be applied without deleting rows; the tasks, epics and
        -- learnings it holds are the operator's work, and whether to lose any
        -- of them is not a decision a launch may take on their behalf. So the
        -- launch stops, the store is stopped with it
        -- (StopTheManagedStoreWhenStartupAborts), and the message says what
        -- happened and what they can do: run a binary whose module matches
        -- the store, or migrate or clear the `dispatch` database themselves.
        --
        -- The store is left running the module it had. Nothing more is started
        -- and nothing is connected to.
}

rule AbortWhenTheModulePublishFails {
    when: ModulePublishFinished(operator, outcome)
    requires: outcome = publish_failed

    ensures: StartupAborted(reason: module_publish_failed)

    @guidance
        -- Any other publish failure — the CLI erroring, the store refusing the
        -- upload, the module failing to initialise. Its own error is attached.
        -- The board does not connect to a store it could not bring up to date:
        -- that store's module may not match the tables this binary subscribes
        -- to, and the failure would surface later and less legibly.
}

rule StopTheManagedStoreWhenTheBoardExits {
    when: BoardExited(operator)
    requires: managed_store_held_by_this_board()

    ensures: ManagedStoreStopped()

    @guidance
        -- THE MANAGED STORE LIVES AS LONG AS THE BOARD, whether this board
        -- started it or adopted it (AdoptAStoreAlreadyOnTheManagedAddress).
        -- Its data stays on disk, and the next launch starts it again over the
        -- same data. BoardExited is the board's normal exit — a confirmed quit
        -- — and also its exit when a later launch retires its window
        -- (RetireTheBoardWindow), which signals it.
        --
        -- A RETIRED BOARD IS SIGNALLED, NOT ASKED: kill-window sends SIGHUP,
        -- whose default disposition ends the process without running an exit.
        -- The board therefore catches SIGHUP and SIGTERM and runs this rule's
        -- stop before it goes, which is what makes "retired" an exit that
        -- stops the store rather than another way to orphan it.
        --
        -- AGENT WINDOWS ARE NOT WAITED FOR. They outlive the board
        -- (RestartingCostsOnlyTheBoard) and lose the store with it; what they
        -- reach through the board is already gone once the board is, and they
        -- reconnect when the next board starts one.
        --
        -- THE BOARD EXITS ONLY ONCE stop_managed_store HAS RETURNED, bounded by
        -- config.managed_store_stop_timeout. That is what keeps a restart
        -- ordered: the launch that retires this board waits until the board
        -- is gone (RetiredMeansGoneNotSignalled), so by then the store is
        -- stopped too, and the new board starts a fresh one rather than
        -- adopting one mid-shutdown. A stop that runs out its timeout leaves
        -- that race open, and its worst case is the new board's first
        -- connection failing with store_unavailable — the next launch works.
}

rule StopTheManagedStoreWhenStartupAborts {
    when: StartupAborted(reason)
    requires: managed_store_held_by_this_board()

    ensures: ManagedStoreStopped()

    @guidance
        -- The abort is an exit like any other, so the same rule applies: a
        -- store this launch started or adopted is stopped before the launch
        -- reports. It covers every abort that can follow the managed store
        -- coming up — the start timing out, either publish failure, the
        -- first connection failing (AbortWhenTheStoreCannotBeReached), and
        -- sync.allium's RefuseToStartWhereOnlySomeChangesReachTheStore — so a
        -- failed launch never leaves a detached store behind it.
        --
        -- Aborts that come before ManagedStoreRequested need no stop: nothing
        -- was held yet.
}

rule AbortWhenTheStoreCannotBeReached {
    when: StoreUnreachableAtStartup(operator, reason)

    ensures: StartupAborted(reason: store_unavailable)

    @guidance
        -- The first attempt failed — sync.allium: OpenBoardConnection says
        -- which ways it can — or ended in an identity conflict. Either way
        -- the board would draw empty and refuse every change, so it does not
        -- draw at all. The reason the attempt gave is part of the message: an
        -- operator told only "unavailable" cannot tell a server that is down
        -- from an address with a typo.
        --
        -- NO RETRY AT STARTUP. The backoff exists for a board that was working
        -- and lost its store; a launch that has never reached it is better
        -- served by an answer now than by a spinner.
}

rule DrawBoardAfterTheHostIsNamed {
    when: StoreReachedAtStartup(operator)
    requires: core/local_host().label != null

    ensures: BoardDrawn()

    @guidance
        -- The last step before the screen is taken, reached only through a
        -- first connection that succeeded (sync.allium: OpenBoardConnection) —
        -- which is itself reached only once the host is named, whether it goes
        -- straight to a named store (ConnectToTheNamedStoreOnceTheHostIsNamed)
        -- or by way of the managed one (BringUpTheManagedStoreOnceTheHostIsNamed
        -- and the module check after it). Both ways of naming it — a machine already
        -- named, and one named a moment ago at the prompt — agree on the only
        -- thing the label gate needs: core/local_host().label is not null. There is no third way in, which is what makes
        -- TheBoardNeverDrawsForAnUnnamedHost a property of the pipeline rather
        -- than a check inside the renderer.
}

rule AbortWhenTheAgentPortIsTaken {
    when: StartupConfigResolved(operator, outcome)
    requires: not agent_port_available

    ensures: StartupAborted(reason: agent_port_unavailable)

    @guidance
        -- The port the agents reach the board on is claimed before the screen
        -- is taken over, and a claim that fails stops the launch with a message
        -- naming the port.
        --
        -- Restarting is what makes this worth stating. The board being replaced
        -- held this port, and a launch that raced its exit used to draw a board
        -- that looked entirely normal and that no agent could reach — the only
        -- report went to a stderr the drawn board had already covered. A launch
        -- that cannot serve agents is not a launch, so it fails where the
        -- operator can read it.
        --
        -- It fails rather than waiting for the port. A retry would be guessing
        -- at how long someone else intends to hold it, and the two causes look
        -- identical from here: a board mid-exit, and a board the operator still
        -- wants running in another session.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Invariants
------------------------------------------------------------

-- ConfigurationIsNeverWrittenWithoutConsent: the operator's configuration
-- directory is modified on exactly one path — an accepted prompt. No other
-- rule here writes to it, and neither the drift check nor a non-interactive
-- run nor a declined prompt may. This is what lets `dispatch tui` be safe to
-- run from a script, and it is the property that removing the standalone setup
-- command would otherwise have quietly cost: with no separate command to
-- decline, an implicit yes on startup would be the only way configuration ever
-- got written. Not expressible as a state assertion — it constrains which
-- transitions may write, not what any single state looks like.

-- ConfigurationDriftNeverBlocksTheBoard: all four StartupConfigOutcome values
-- lead to the board drawing. A stale skill, an unwritable file, a declined
-- prompt and an absent operator are each reported and then stepped over. The
-- board's own function does not depend on any of the four artefacts being
-- current, so refusing to start over one would withhold a working tool to
-- enforce a cosmetic property.

-- ExactlyOneBoardPerSession: a session named by config.session_name holds at
-- most one board. Retiring the board's window before starting a new one is
-- what upholds it — every launch removes the previous board before it becomes
-- a rival — and nothing in this spec creates a second session under a derived
-- or suffixed name. Two boards would contend for one agent port and one set of
-- tmux keybindings.

-- EveryLaunchProducesAFreshBoard: `dispatch tui` starts a board, on every path
-- through this spec that does not abort. There is no path on which the command
-- succeeds and the board the operator ends up looking at is one that was
-- already running. This is the property that makes the command mean the same
-- thing the first time and the fiftieth: the operator reaches for it precisely
-- when the board they have is not the one they want, and a launch that quietly
-- reattaches answers a question they did not ask.

-- ALaunchNeverRetiresItsOwnWindow: no path retires the window the launching
-- process is running in. Two rules uphold it from opposite sides — the started
-- board skips the retire entirely, and a shell sharing the board's window is
-- refused before one is attempted — and they are stated separately because the
-- remedies differ: one is the ordinary case and says nothing, the other is an
-- operator mistake and must say so. A retire that reached its own window would
-- close the caller, and where that window was the session's last, discard the
-- session too. Not expressible as a state assertion: it constrains what a
-- transition may target, not what any state looks like.

-- RestartingCostsOnlyTheBoard: retiring a board never ends an agent's session.
-- The windows dispatch opened for agents, editors and shells are outside what
-- a launch may close, and a restart therefore costs the operator a redraw
-- rather than their running work. Not expressible as a state assertion — it
-- constrains what a transition may destroy, not what any state looks like.

-- StartupAbortsOnlyOnAnUnusableSubstrate: of the startup conditions this spec
-- describes, exactly seven abort, and each is something the board needs and
-- cannot obtain: a tmux session (absent tmux, or one that refuses); a session
-- it can name, so that closing a window closes one it owns; a board window it
-- may retire without closing its own caller, or that will close at all; the
-- port agents reach it on; an identity for the machine it is running on; a
-- name for that identity; and the shared store every row it draws and every
-- change it makes lives in — the one the operator named, or, when they named
-- none, the managed one it runs for itself. Every other condition degrades,
-- and a future startup step that wants to abort belongs in this invariant —
-- count and list both — or it does not belong at startup.
--
-- The fourteen values of StartupAbortReason cover those seven: tmux_unavailable
-- and launch_rejected are the two ways the first fails, and the launch path
-- cannot tell them apart without asking, which is the distinction
-- DistinguishesAbsentFromBroken exists for; and six are ways the last fails.
-- store_unavailable is the store — named or managed — refusing the first
-- connection; the other five (spacetime_cli_missing, managed_store_port_taken,
-- managed_store_did_not_start, module_needs_manual_migration,
-- module_publish_failed) are the managed store failing to come up in a state
-- the board can use, for the reasons given beside StartupAbortReason.
--
-- A store that is merely absent is no longer among them. Until task #12296 a
-- launch naming no store aborted (store_unconfigured); that condition is now
-- one the board repairs for itself, which is what this invariant asks of every
-- condition that can be.
--
-- The store is the newest substrate and was not one until Phase 12 of the
-- SpacetimeDB migration (task #4916): before it, a board with no store read
-- and wrote its own database and was a first-class way to run. With the store
-- mandatory, a board that could not reach it would draw no rows and refuse
-- every change, which is not a degraded board but an empty one.
--
-- The last two are the newest and the least obviously a substrate, so they are
-- argued rather than asserted. `host_unnamed` fires only when this machine has
-- never been named AND nobody is there to name it (see
-- AbortWhenTheHostIsUnnamedAndNoOneCanAnswer). It is not "a configuration
-- artefact is stale", which degrades; it is "the board cannot say which machine
-- it is", and every task this board would dispatch is stamped with that answer.
-- Unlike the others it is fixable without touching the environment — one
-- interactive launch resolves it permanently — which is why it can be the only
-- abort reason that asks a human for something rather than reporting an
-- obstacle.
--
-- `host_identity_unavailable` is the stronger form of the same condition and
-- the clearer substrate of the two: the store the identity lives in is
-- unusable, either because the identity could not be read or minted at all
-- (AbortWhenTheHostIdentityStoreIsUnusable) — so there is not even an unnamed
-- machine to speak of — or because an unnamed machine's label was handed over
-- and could not be written back (startup.allium: NameHostFromStartupPrompt),
-- which leaves the launch exactly as unable to name the machine as the first
-- does. Two rules ensure the one reason, one per trigger that can discover the
-- store is broken, rather than one rule with a guard spanning both — see
-- NameHostFromStartupPrompt's guidance for why. It is the one abort in this
-- list whose
-- alternative is not a degraded board but a corrupted database. A launch that
-- carried on would claim tasks — the claim's locality gate passes anything no
-- machine holds, which is every task that has never been dispatched, and an
-- undeterminable identity does not narrow that arm — provision worktrees for
-- them, and then skip the stamp that records which machine owns the worktree,
-- for want of an id. The residue is rows that
-- violate core.allium's HostTracksWorktree, written durably and invisibly. It
-- does not ask whether anybody can answer, because no answer exists: the
-- remedy is repairing the store, not naming the machine.
--
-- Two conditions outside this spec's scope still abort, and neither weakens the
-- above: an unavailable home directory, which observability.allium's
-- `AnUnavailableHomeDirectoryIsAFailureNotAPath` requires to fail rather than
-- compose into a path, and a database that will not open, which is the board's
-- own substrate rather than a step on the way to it. Both precede any question
-- this spec answers.

-- TheBoardNeverDrawsForAnUnnamedHost: BoardDrawn() is reachable only through
-- HostLabelResolved, and both rules that emit it hold core/local_host().label
-- != null — one because it found a label, the other because it has just had one
-- written. There is no path on which the board draws for a machine nobody has
-- named, and no non-interactive escape hatch: the launch that cannot ask aborts
-- (AbortWhenTheHostIsUnnamedAndNoOneCanAnswer) rather than proceeding with a
-- placeholder. Nor is there an escape hatch below the label: a launch whose
-- host identity store is unusable aborts too
-- (AbortWhenTheHostIdentityStoreIsUnusable), so "the board never draws without
-- knowing which machine it is" holds at both depths rather than only where a
-- label was missing. That covers the label the operator has just typed as well
-- as the one that was never there: NameHostFromStartupPrompt's own `if` is
-- exhaustive over the state RenameHost leaves behind — a label that persisted
-- emits HostLabelResolved, and a persist that did not take emits
-- StartupAborted directly, in the same rule, rather than falling through to a
-- HostLabelResolved neither branch produced. There is no path on which
-- HostLabelResolved is emitted for a label the store did not keep.
-- CheckHostLabel's own arms are exhaustive and disjoint on the identity itself
-- — no identity at all, or an identity whose label is then examined — so there
-- is no third reading of the gate in which it falls through silently. This is
-- deliberately stronger than
-- ConfigurationDriftNeverBlocksTheBoard, which is the opposite property for the
-- check immediately before it: configuration is a convenience the board can do
-- without, a host label is not. Not expressible as a state assertion — it
-- constrains which transitions may reach a drawn board, not what any single
-- state looks like.

-- ANamedStoreIsNeverManaged: when the launch names a store
-- (explicit_store_server() != null), no rule starts, publishes to, records a
-- module hash in, or stops anything — the managed-store rules all require
-- explicit_store_server() = null, and managed_store_held_by_this_board() is
-- false on such a launch. A named store may be somebody else's, and a board
-- that published its module over a shared team store, or stopped one on quit,
-- would be doing to colleagues what no launch may do to the operator. Naming
-- a store — even the managed address itself, spelled out — is how the operator
-- opts out of management.

-- TheManagedStoreStopsWithItsBoard: every exit this board can run —
-- a confirmed quit, being retired by a later launch, and every startup abort
-- after ManagedStoreRequested — stops a managed store this board holds. The
-- store is never left running by a board that could run its exit, so there is
-- one lifetime to reason about — the board's — and the only store found
-- already serving the managed address is one a board could not stop, or one
-- somebody started by hand. Not expressible as a state assertion: it
-- constrains what an exit transition must do.

-- TheManagedStoreRunsTheEmbeddedModule: no connection to a managed store is
-- attempted unless the store's recorded module hash equals this binary's
-- embedded one and the store holds config.managed_database_name — because it
-- already did (ConnectWhenTheManagedModuleIsCurrent)
-- or because the embedded module has just been published and its hash recorded
-- (ConnectOnceTheModuleIsPublished). Every other publish outcome aborts. So a
-- managed board never subscribes against a schema other than the one it was
-- built with, which is the class of failure the hand-run `spacetime publish`
-- step left to the operator to remember.

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Surfaces
------------------------------------------------------------

surface BoardLaunchCommand {
    facing operator: User

    -- `dispatch tui [--spacetime-server <url>] [--db <path>] [--port <n>]` —
    -- the one command that starts dispatch. The store may instead be named by
    -- DISPATCH_SPACETIME_SERVER. Naming one is optional: without either, the
    -- board runs its own managed store on config.managed_store_address
    -- (BringUpTheManagedStoreOnceTheHostIsNamed), which needs the `spacetime`
    -- CLI on PATH (AbortWhenTheManagedStoreHasNoCli). With either, that store
    -- is used as it is (ANamedStoreIsNeverManaged).

    contracts:
        demands ConfigDriftEngine
        demands SessionLauncher
        demands ManagedStoreHost

    provides:
        LaunchBoard(operator)

    @guarantee OneCommandFromAColdMachine
        -- Run on a machine with no tmux session, no dispatch configuration and
        -- no store — only tmux and the `spacetime` CLI installed — this
        -- command reaches a drawn board without the operator running anything
        -- else first. Supplying the session is silent; supplying the store is
        -- silent too, on loopback, starting it and publishing the embedded
        -- module only where needed, and stopping it again when the board
        -- exits; supplying the configuration asks; naming the machine asks and
        -- will not take no for an answer, but asks once ever and arrives
        -- pre-filled with the hostname.
        --
        -- The store is supplied only when none is named. A named store is a
        -- server that may be somebody else's, so it is still a precondition
        -- the operator provides — running, and running a module this binary
        -- can subscribe to.

    @guarantee TheManagedStoreKeepsItsData
        -- Stopping the managed store on exit, adopting one already running,
        -- and publishing a new module to it never discard what it holds. A
        -- schema change that could only be applied by deleting data stops the
        -- launch and says so (AbortWhenTheModuleCannotMigrateAutomatically);
        -- the operator decides what happens to their data, not the launch.

    @guarantee ASecondLaunchRestartsTheBoard
        -- Running the command again, with dispatch's session already there,
        -- puts the operator in front of a board this invocation started. A
        -- board left over from a previous run is closed first, whether it had
        -- exited, was still drawing, or was built from a binary since replaced.

    @guarantee AgentWindowsSurviveARestart
        -- Restarting the board leaves every other window in the session
        -- running. The agents the operator dispatched are still there when the
        -- new board draws.

    @guarantee ArgumentsSurviveTheSessionHandoff
        -- The database path and port the operator passed are the ones the board
        -- comes up with, whether or not a session had to be created to hold it.

    @guarantee NoSeparateSetupCommand
        -- There is no second command to run before this one. The drift check
        -- makes it unnecessary, and leaving it in place would leave two ways to
        -- write the same artefacts that could answer differently.
        --
        -- Removing configuration is not the same shape of problem and keeps its
        -- own command: it is rare, destructive, and has nowhere natural to live
        -- in front of a board that is about to draw.
}

surface ConfigUpdatePrompt {
    facing operator: User

    -- Shown in the terminal before the board takes the screen, on the one
    -- launch where something is stale.

    exposes:
        for item in ConfigDrift.items:
            item

    provides:
        ConfigUpdateAccepted(operator, drift)
        ConfigUpdateDeclined(operator, drift)

    @guarantee NamesWhatWillChange
        -- The prompt lists the stale artefacts. The operator is never asked to
        -- approve an unnamed set of writes.

    @guarantee AppearsBeforeTheBoardTakesTheScreen
        -- The prompt is answered on an ordinary terminal, not over a drawn
        -- board. The board draws only after the prompt resolves.

    @guarantee AbsentWhenNothingIsStale
        -- No prompt, and no output at all, on a launch with a current
        -- configuration.
}

surface StartupConfigReport {
    facing operator: User

    -- The non-interactive counterpart of the prompt: what a script sees.

    exposes:
        for item in ConfigDrift.items:
            item

    @guarantee ReportsWithoutWriting
        -- It states what is out of date and changes nothing. The operator can
        -- act on it later from an interactive launch.

    @guarantee SilentWhenClean
        -- Nothing is printed when nothing is stale, interactive or not.
}

surface HostLabelPrompt {
    facing operator: User

    -- Shown in the terminal before the board takes the screen, on the one
    -- launch where this machine has no name yet. The only surface in the system
    -- that sets a host label (host.allium declares none — see the note in its
    -- Surfaces section).

    exposes:
        -- The machine's hostname, pre-filled as the answer, so accepting the
        -- sensible default is one keypress.
        machine_hostname()

    provides:
        HostLabelSubmitted(operator, label)
            when trim(label) != ""

    @guarantee ThereIsNoWayPast
        -- The prompt offers no decline, no skip and no empty answer. Unlike
        -- ConfigUpdatePrompt, which has a second action for saying no, this
        -- surface provides exactly one — because the board does not draw until
        -- it is answered, and an action that cannot lead anywhere would be an
        -- offer to hang the launch.

    @guarantee AppearsOnceEver
        -- A named machine never sees it again. It is the first launch's prompt,
        -- not a recurring one, and nothing re-opens it: changing the name later
        -- needs a rename surface, which does not exist yet (host.allium:
        -- RenameHost is the rule waiting for one).

    @guarantee AppearsBeforeTheBoardTakesTheScreen
        -- Answered on an ordinary terminal, after the configuration prompt and
        -- before the first frame, so the operator is never typing a machine name
        -- over a drawn board.

    @guarantee SaysWhyItIsAsking
        -- The prompt states what the name is for — this machine is about to own
        -- worktrees that other people's boards will attribute to it — rather
        -- than presenting an unexplained text field on the one launch the
        -- operator has least context for.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open Questions
------------------------------------------------------------


open question "ACCEPTED (task #12296) as an edge, not built against: what becomes of the managed store when the board cannot run its exit — a crash, a panic that aborts the process, SIGKILL, the machine losing power, or the tmux server being killed with the board in it? StopTheManagedStoreWhenTheBoardExits cannot fire, so the detached store keeps running until something stops it. The next launch adopts it (AdoptAStoreAlreadyOnTheManagedAddress) and its exit stops it, so nothing is lost and nothing duplicates — but between the two, a store nobody owns runs on loopback. Is that acceptable, or should something (a pidfile checked at launch, a parent-death signal, a watchdog in the store's launcher) bound how long an orphaned store can outlive its board?"

open question "ACCEPTED (task #12296) as an edge the operator reaches only on purpose: two boards on one machine in two tmux sessions (reachable on purpose — see config.board_window_name) on two agent ports both run managed: the second adopts the store the first started, and whichever exits first stops the store under the other, which then shows an outage it cannot recover from until relaunched. Should an exit stop the managed store only when no other board still holds it, should a second managed board on one machine be refused, or is this accepted as an edge the operator reaches only on purpose?"

open question "STILL PLACEHOLDERS (task #12296 measured nothing beyond a near-empty data directory, where a start-and-publish took well under a second): config.managed_store_start_timeout (15 seconds) and config.managed_store_stop_timeout (5 seconds) are placeholders, not measured values. What does a cold `spacetime start` over a large data directory actually take, and how long may a quit be held up waiting for the store to stop?"
